WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.770 --> 00:00:03.120
Sonic the Hedgehog is off to the races.

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:07.190
After a troubled production history that involved
a complete redesign of the title character

00:00:07.190 --> 00:00:11.959
due to the first trailer giving fans nightmares,
the first live-action vehicle for the speedy

00:00:11.959 --> 00:00:15.620
blue hedgehog from the iconic Sega video game
series is here.

00:00:15.620 --> 00:00:18.420
The general critical consensus: not too shabby.

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:23.020
Sonic famously had its release date pushed
back from late last year due to the redesign,

00:00:23.020 --> 00:00:28.350
which became necessary after fans took to
social media in droves to express their bewilderment,

00:00:28.350 --> 00:00:33.780
disappointment, and abject terror at Sonic's
weird eyes, ripped thighs, and bizarrely human

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:34.780
teeth.

00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:38.820
Fortunately for the filmmakers, the VFX shots
featuring Sonic in the trailer were the only

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:43.520
ones that had been finished, so it wasn't
a complete overhaul; studio Paramount had

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:47.240
to shell out a fair amount of dough to make
it happen, though, so the fact that critics

00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:51.559
are so far pleased with Sonic has got to be
a relief for the studio's brass.

00:00:51.559 --> 00:00:55.420
"They pulled out all my teeth!

00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:56.710
It's perfect!"

00:00:56.710 --> 00:01:01.620
The movie is Certified Fresh on Rotten Tomatoes,
and the positive notices generally agree that

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:06.359
while Sonic is far from perfect, it's a breezy
good time which benefits from a quick pace,

00:01:06.359 --> 00:01:07.950
which is, really, only fitting.

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:10.100
"How are you not dead?"

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:11.819
"I have no idea."

00:01:11.819 --> 00:01:16.279
It's not even necessary to be a fan of the
video game series to enjoy Sonic, as noted

00:01:16.279 --> 00:01:19.320
by Johnny Oleksinski of the New York Post,
who wrote,

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:22.399
"The movie is a lot better than it deserves
to be.

00:01:22.399 --> 00:01:26.959
You'll actually care about what happens to
the prickly blue dude, even if you never cared

00:01:26.959 --> 00:01:32.219
about getting to zone seven...Sonic the Hedgehog
is very much in conversation with Pokémon:

00:01:32.219 --> 00:01:33.780
Detective Pikachu."

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:38.619
A fair number of critics, such as the Washington
Post's Kristen Page-Kirby, seemed genuinely

00:01:38.619 --> 00:01:41.009
shocked to be turning in a positive review.

00:01:41.009 --> 00:01:45.630
The headline of her review contained a double-take,
reading, quote, "Sonic the Hedgehog is sweet,

00:01:45.630 --> 00:01:46.999
funny, and smart.

00:01:46.999 --> 00:01:47.999
Wait, what?"

00:01:47.999 --> 00:01:49.869
The author praised Sonic as:

00:01:49.869 --> 00:01:54.530
"Quippy without being mean, and sweet without
being sappy, making [it] a trip that's well

00:01:54.530 --> 00:01:56.179
worth taking."

00:01:56.179 --> 00:02:00.739
In general, the flick's positive reviews found
it to be a fun, family-friendly, action-packed

00:02:00.739 --> 00:02:02.909
ride with a distinct '90s vibe.

00:02:02.909 --> 00:02:05.520
The Weekend Warrior's Edward Douglas put it
like this:

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:09.980
"Is it possible that Sonic the Hedgehog is
the first thoroughly entertaining movie of

00:02:09.980 --> 00:02:10.980
the year?

00:02:10.980 --> 00:02:11.980
Yes, indeedy."

00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:16.000
Negative reviews, meanwhile, found Sonic the
Hedgehog to be just mediocre.

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:20.060
Those who disagreed with the majority found
Sonic to be a halfhearted effort, not to mention

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:24.870
derivative of better films, such as E.T.,
and devoid of a unique sense of humor.

00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:27.790
Referring to Sonic's infamous original design,
A.A.

00:02:27.790 --> 00:02:28.790
Dowd of The A.V.

00:02:28.790 --> 00:02:29.790
Club wrote,

00:02:29.790 --> 00:02:34.070
"[When Sonic] opens his mouth...you maybe
wish those ghastly teeth were still there,

00:02:34.070 --> 00:02:39.350
if only to distract from the lazy wisecracks,
pop culture references, and earnest clichés

00:02:39.350 --> 00:02:44.730
that come flooding out...It's that kind of
movie: a nattering chore of a 'family' comedy

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:49.100
that feels written by committee and directed
by an indifferent machine."

00:02:49.100 --> 00:02:54.170
Some negative notices at least conceded that
kids, as always, will probably be into Sonic,

00:02:54.170 --> 00:02:57.340
even if it may leave their parents eyeing
the exit sign.

00:02:57.340 --> 00:02:59.750
USA Today's Brian Truitt wrote,

00:02:59.750 --> 00:03:03.190
"The film is fine, familiar fare for gamers
and children.

00:03:03.190 --> 00:03:08.200
Sonic sprints, [Jim] Carrey mugs, but the
creative juices run out quickly."

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:12.470
In some critics, though, the flick simply
engendered outright hostility.

00:03:12.470 --> 00:03:14.970
Evan Dossey of Midwest Film Journal wrote,

00:03:14.970 --> 00:03:19.601
"It's been a long road for Sonic the Hedgehog
to the silver screen, considering it's a 30-year

00:03:19.601 --> 00:03:23.090
old intellectual property that hasn't been
relevant for two decades.

00:03:23.090 --> 00:03:29.050
A long, crazy, talent-sinking road that leads
to nowhere...Sonic the Hedgehog joins an endless

00:03:29.050 --> 00:03:34.660
stream of attempted IP resurrections destined
to litter the bottom of the box office, forgotten

00:03:34.660 --> 00:03:37.480
until their inevitable reboot or re-quel."

00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:42.290
Regardless of whether or not the movie was
their cup of tea, critics agreed near-unanimously

00:03:42.290 --> 00:03:47.500
on one thing: Jim Carrey turns in an unrestrained,
manic performance, the likes of which we haven't

00:03:47.500 --> 00:03:48.500
seen for decades.

00:03:48.500 --> 00:03:49.500
"Oh!

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:51.380
Give me a big!

00:03:51.380 --> 00:03:52.380
Fat!

00:03:52.380 --> 00:03:53.380
Break!"

00:03:53.380 --> 00:03:58.620
In a positive review, Total Film's Jordan
Farley called Carrey's performance "preposterous,"

00:03:58.620 --> 00:04:03.180
adding that adults in the audience would find
it to be, quote, "an enjoyable shot of nostalgia."

00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:05.569
Travis Hopson of Punch Drunk Critics wrote,

00:04:05.569 --> 00:04:10.040
"Whatever message the movie might have is
secondary to Carrey who dominates literally

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:15.180
every moment he's on screen, giving such an
exaggerated, wacky performance it makes Fire

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:17.819
Marshall Bill look positively tame."

00:04:17.819 --> 00:04:22.659
In other words, Sonic the Hedgehog looks like
a heck of a lot of fun, perhaps surprisingly.

00:04:22.659 --> 00:04:26.590
If nothing else, it deserves to be remembered
as a product of that time when fans chimed

00:04:26.590 --> 00:04:30.969
in with their two cents on social media about
how a film could be made better, and the filmmakers

00:04:30.969 --> 00:04:32.220
actually listened.

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:37.460
Regardless of how you may feel about Sonic,
that in itself is a minor miracle.

